@startuml Basic Facade Pattern

' Define the styles
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam classBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam arrowColor #6c757d
skinparam stereotypeCBackgroundColor #e9ecef

' Title
title Basic Facade Pattern - Core Structure

' Facade class
class Facade <<Facade>> {
  -subsystemA: SubsystemA
  -subsystemB: SubsystemB
  -subsystemC: SubsystemC
  +operation1(): void
  +operation2(): void
}

' Subsystem Classes
class SubsystemA <<SubsystemClass>> {
  +operationA1(): void
  +operationA2(): void
  +operationA3(): void
}

class SubsystemB <<SubsystemClass>> {
  +operationB1(): void
  +operationB2(): void
  +operationB3(): void
}

class SubsystemC <<SubsystemClass>> {
  +operationC1(): void
  +operationC2(): void
  +operationC3(): void
}

' Client
class Client <<Client>> {
  -facade: Facade
  +doWork(): void
}

' Relationships
Facade o--> SubsystemA : uses
Facade o--> SubsystemB : uses
Facade o--> SubsystemC : uses

Client --> Facade : uses

' Pattern component notes
note right of Facade
  <b>Facade</b>

  Provides a unified, simplified interface
  to a set of interfaces in a subsystem.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Knows which subsystem classes are
    responsible for a request
  • Delegates client requests to appropriate
    subsystem objects
  • Provides high-level methods that combine
    multiple subsystem operations

  <b>Key Method Example:</b>
  operation1() might internally call:
    subsystemA.operationA1()
    subsystemB.operationB2()
    subsystemC.operationC1()

  This hides complexity from the client.
end note

note left of SubsystemA
  <b>Subsystem Class A</b>

  Implements subsystem functionality.
  Performs actual work but is complex
  to use directly.

  <b>Characteristics:</b>
  • Handles a portion of the work
  • Has no knowledge of the facade
  • Can be used independently if needed
  • Often has many methods and dependencies

  Multiple subsystem classes work together
  to provide complete functionality.
end note

note right of Client
  <b>Client</b>

  Uses the facade instead of interacting
  directly with subsystem classes.

  <b>Benefits:</b>
  • Simplified interface to complex subsystem
  • Reduced dependencies on subsystem classes
  • Easier to use and understand
  • Loosely coupled to subsystem

  <b>Usage Pattern:</b>
  client.facade.operation1()

  Instead of:
  subsystemA.operationA1()
  subsystemB.operationB2()
  subsystemC.operationC1()
end note

' Pattern flow note
note bottom of Facade
  <b>Facade Pattern Flow:</b>

  1. Client calls facade.operation1()
  2. Facade delegates to SubsystemA.operationA1()
  3. Facade delegates to SubsystemB.operationB2()
  4. Facade delegates to SubsystemC.operationC1()
  5. Facade returns result to client

  <b>Key Advantage:</b>
  Client only needs to know about one class (Facade)
  instead of three subsystem classes (A, B, C).

  <b>Important:</b>
  Subsystem classes remain accessible - clients can
  still use them directly if they need fine-grained
  control. The facade is an optional convenience.
end note

' Legend
note right of SubsystemC
  <b>Facade Pattern Core Components:</b>

  <b>Facade:</b> Provides a simple interface to a
  complex subsystem. Knows which subsystem classes
  handle which requests and delegates accordingly.

  <b>Subsystem Classes:</b> Implement subsystem
  functionality. Handle work assigned by the facade.
  Have no knowledge of the facade; they keep no
  reference to it.

  <b>Client:</b> Uses the facade for simplified
  access. Can still access subsystem directly if
  needed for advanced use cases.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Simplifies complex subsystems
  • Reduces coupling between clients and subsystems
  • Provides a single entry point
  • Doesn't prevent access to subsystem if needed
  • Makes the subsystem easier to use
  • Promotes loose coupling

  <b>Intent:</b>
  Provide a unified interface to a set of interfaces
  in a subsystem. Facade defines a higher-level
  interface that makes the subsystem easier to use.

  <b>Real-World Analogy:</b>
  Like a receptionist at a company who directs you
  to the right departments instead of you having to
  know the entire organizational structure.
end note

@enduml